Overlay patch 0039: the flat-screen-context latch (VR-spec D3,
VR-DONOR-MAP §2). The donor's biggest surprise is that OoT needs no
enumerated table of 2D contexts -- it is one line:

    s32 vrFlatScreen = (gPlayState == NULL) || (gPlayState->pauseCtx.state != 0);

"no PlayState at all" (title, file select, name entry, the debug select menu)
"or the kaleido pause menu is up". Shops, dialogue, cutscenes, the ocarina,
the item roulette all render as normal stereo world.

The architecturally load-bearing part is WHERE it is evaluated, not what it
says. It is LATCHED at the tick boundary, immediately before GameState_Update
builds this frame's display list, so a DL is never interpreted in a different
mode than the one it was built for (the donor's VR_ApplyModeRequest latch --
its best structural idea). Reading the predicate later, from inside the
interpreter or from the compositor thread, would let the mode flip mid-frame.

Two values are published:
  gSohVRFlatLatch  -- the latched predicate for THIS frame's DL. 0031 reads it
                      and keeps the game's own matrix (mono, flat) instead of
                      substituting the per-eye A.V.P, so a flat frame lands on
                      the panel rather than being smeared across the world.
  gSohVRFlatRaw    -- the live predicate, for diagnostics (`vr mode`) only.

Published unconditionally (not gated on gSohVRMode) so `vr mode` can report
the predicate before VR is ever entered -- it costs one pointer test per tick
and nothing reads it outside VR. __IOS__-only.

rev2 (VR R2a, device round): PRE-RENDERED ROOMS JOIN THE PREDICATE.
Overlay 0040 flipped SoH's "Disable 2D Pre-Rendered Scenes" enhancement on in
VR, following the donor, which ships it default-on. SoH's own tooltip says what
that actually needs: "Enable this when using a mod that implements 3D backdrops
for these areas." Without such a mod the room draws its bare placeholder mesh,
which is why the user's Link's house was an all-green box on 1.0.1.1 (his
Documents/mods holds a 4K TEXTURE pack, not a 3D-backdrop mod).

The room's own header says which kind of room it is: MeshHeader type 1 is the
image-backed ("pre-rendered background") shape, drawn by func_80096F6C through
Room_DrawBackground2D as a SCREEN-SPACE image (z_room.c). A screen-space image
has no disparity: through 0031's ortho gate it lands identical in both eyes,
i.e. a flat painting glued to the head while Link moves in 3D in front of it.

So VR gets a three-way setting, gSohVRRoomMode, published by the shell:
  0 panel (DEFAULT) -- the whole room is treated as a flat-screen context and
      renders mono onto the world-locked panel, exactly like file select. The
      pre-rendered art is shown as authored, world-locked rather than
      head-glued, and the transition is one the player already knows.
  1 flat  -- the backdrop is drawn per eye (the head-glued painting above).
  2 3d    -- SoH's enhancement, for users who install a 3D-backdrop mod.
Only mode 0 touches this latch, and only inside VR: outside VR (gSohVRMode 0)
the predicate is byte-for-byte what rev1 published, so the flat window and the
3D panel cannot regress.

rev3 (VR R3) -- THE RIGHT STICK BECOMES THE TURN, AND ONLY THE TURN. This is
the fix for the user's 1.0.1.3 report: "when i snap turn, it stops my controller
from working properly, where i can't move anymore, and i can't perform actions,
except i can still keep snap turning."

The mechanism, measured rather than guessed. LUS binds the right stick TWICE.
Ship::ControllerDefaultMappings maps RIGHTX/RIGHTY to the right stick axes
(ControllerDefaultMappings.cpp:125-129), and the LUS subclass ALSO maps them to
the C-BUTTONS -- RIGHTY- = C-up, RIGHTY+ = C-down, RIGHTX- = C-left, RIGHTX+ =
C-right (libultraship/.../LUS ControllerDefaultMappings.cpp:94-102) -- at the
global axis threshold of 25% (GlobalSDLDeviceSettings.cpp:11) of MAX_AXIS_RANGE
85, i.e. raw 21. R2b's snap turn armed at 0.6 (raw 51). So every turn flick
pressed C a frame or two BEFORE it turned, and was still holding C when the turn
disarmed: C-up is first-person look, C-left/right/down are item use, and any of
them puts Link in a state that eats the left stick and the face buttons. Turning
kept working because it was read out of Play_Draw, which runs in every one of
those states -- exactly the asymmetry the user described.

rev3 fixes it where spec D9 says input decisions belong: at the ONE merged
snapshot, at the tick boundary, before Play_Update, so Player, the camera and
free-look all see the same corrected bytes.

  (a) PUBLISH gSohVRTurnAxis -- the right stick x as a level in -1..1. The shell
      owns the single edge detector and the single integrator, on the loop
      thread, where dt is real and the turn pivots on the LIVE head (donor
      vr_apply_snap_turn, vr_openxr.cpp:672-682). SMOOTH is the default style
      (donor style 1); snap is the option. the user: "SMOOTH should be an option
      instead of snap turning ... and smooth turning should be the default."
  (b) CLEAR the C bits the right stick just set, by exactly the provenance test
      the mapping used (past 25% of MAX_AXIS_RANGE on that axis, that direction)
      -- so a C-button a user has bound to an actual BUTTON still gets through.
      rev5 (VR R5): and so does a C-button the SENSE PAIR set. Overlay 0047 ORs
      the Sense six-input taxonomy into the pad EARLIER, in padmgr, and R4
      recorded the resulting collision honestly rather than fixing it: a
      Sense-produced C-left arriving on the same tick as a right-stick
      deflection past 25% left was cleared with the stick's, because this mask
      worked by BIT and the two are indistinguishable once merged. They are NOT
      indistinguishable at the source -- 0047 publishes exactly what it OR'd in
      as gSohVRSensePadBits -- so the mask is now by PROVENANCE: subtract the
      Sense-sourced bits (this tick's and, for the release edge, the previous
      tick's) from the stick-driven set before masking anything. A bit the
      Sense set is never cleared, whatever the stick is doing.
  (c) ZERO right_stick_x/y in the snapshot, which also takes the stick away from
      free-look (z_camera.c:1421,1504) and from RightStickAim (z_player.c:12633)
      -- in VR the head IS the camera, so there is nothing for them to do.
  (d) REMAP THE D-PAD TO THE C-BUTTONS. Without this, (b) and (c) would leave VR
      with no way to use a C item at all. The D-pad is unused in OoT gameplay,
      so in VR it becomes the C-pad, edges included (press/rel are taken from
      the D-pad's own edge bits, which are already correct).
  (e) SUPPRESS the turn while the ocarina is out. Audio_GetOcaInput
      (code_800EC960.c:1302) calls PadMgr_RequestPadData AGAIN -- a genuine
      second snapshot in vanilla that this block cannot reach -- so the right
      stick still plays notes there. Rather than fight it, VR stops turning
      while the ocarina is out so the two cannot contend. rev8 fixes WHICH test
      says "out": `msgCtx.ocarinaMode` is a LATCH that stays non-zero until the
      next textbox, so the VR turn used to die for the rest of a session after
      one ocarina use; the predicate is now msgMode in 0x09..0x33. HONEST GAP: with
      "CustomOcarina.RightStick" on, the right stick plays notes and does not
      turn; that is the vanilla binding and R3 leaves it alone.

All of (a)-(e) are gated on VR gameplay -- gSohVRMode != 0 and the latch this
same block just computed being 0. The flat window, the 3D panel, the pause menu
and every iPhone build see the pad byte for byte as before.

rev9 (VR R16 part B): THE MARKET IS A PANEL TOO -- FIXED CAMERAS JOIN THE
PREDICATE. the user, wearing 1.0.1.20: in the Market it "wasn't the 2D panel as
it should be, wasn't rendered properly, and hard to get out."

rev2's arm is the only pre-rendered test in this tree and it asks the ROOM:
meshHeader type 1, the image-backed room shape. The Market is not one. Measured
straight out of the user's own oot.o2r: market_day/night/ruins carry NO
Background resource at all -- their rooms are ordinary 3D geometry -- while
their scene collision carries CAM_SET_PREREND_PIVOT (0x1A) and, in the day
scene, CAM_SET_MARKET_BALCONY (0x14). The Market's "pre-rendered" look is
CAMERA data, not room data: geometry authored to be seen from one fixed angle.
In stereo, with a camera that refuses to follow the head, that is exactly what
the user described. (The back alley, the Market entrance, the Temple of Time
exterior, the houses and the shops all DO carry Background resources, so rev2's
room arm already latches them; this arm adds nothing there.)

SoH's own set of fixed-camera settings is DisableFixedCamera.cpp:86-89 --
CAM_SET_PREREND_FIXED, CAM_SET_PREREND_PIVOT, CAM_SET_PIVOT_FROM_SIDE,
CAM_SET_MARKET_BALCONY -- plus CAM_SET_PREREND_SIDE_SCROLL, which the same o2r
scan finds only in hairal_niwa (the castle courtyard guards). We do NOT
re-enable that enhancement: R1 took a SIGSEGV inside Camera_Normal1 with it
forced on (OTRGlobals.cpp ~1080-1090, overlay 0040 rev2). This is a read of the
active camera's setting and nothing else.

SCENE-GATED, because the setting alone is too broad. The same scan finds those
settings live in a dungeon (jyasinzou, the Spirit Temple, 5 regions), in the
Lost Woods (spot10), in the Sacred Forest Meadow (spot05), on the castle
grounds (spot15), in the courtyard (hairal_niwa, 9 side-scroll regions) and in
half a dozen minigame rooms. Turning a Spirit Temple region or a Lost Woods
clearing into a flat panel as the player walks across a camera boundary is a
worse bug than the one being fixed. So the arm is gated on the TOWN scenes --
SoH's own skyboxSceneControlList (Disable2DBackgrounds.cpp:46-60) minus the
courtyard and the Forest Temple: the Market entrance, the back alley, the
Market and the Temple of Time exterior, day / night / ruins.

DEBOUNCED, in the spirit of 0031's size hold: the active camera's setting flips
as Link crosses a camera region, and a latch that flips with it would strobe the
whole frame between panel and stereo. The arm must hold its new value for
SOH_VR_FIXEDCAM_HOLD consecutive ticks before the latch follows it.

ONE TICK OLD, said out loud: gSohVRCamSetting is published by overlay 0037 from
inside Camera_Update, which runs in Play_Update -- i.e. AFTER this block. The
value read here is therefore the setting the PREVIOUS tick's camera had. With a
multi-tick debounce in front of it that is a rounding error on the transition,
and it is never wrong about which arm fired.

Published as gSohVRRoomFixedCam beside gSohVRRoomImage so `vr room` says which
arm latched the frame.

rev6 (VR R6) adds one thing that is not about the pad at all, and it is here for
the THREAD rather than the topic: the overlay-0050 hand-matrix table is cleared
in this block, because this block runs once per frame ON THE GAME THREAD, which
is the only thread that writes that table. R6's first attempt cleared it from
the compositor loop thread at VR exit -- the same two-owner mistake as the
gSohVROverlayDL crash this round diagnosed, milder only because the consumers are
bounds-checked. The loop thread now asks; this clears. See the comment at the
site.

--- a/soh/src/code/graph.c
+++ b/soh/src/code/graph.c
@@ -295,6 +295,323 @@
     CLOSE_DISPS(gfxCtx);
 
     GameState_ReqPadData(gameState);
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    /* SOH_IOS (overlay 0039, VR-spec D3 / VR-DONOR-MAP 2): latch the
+     * flat-screen predicate at the TICK BOUNDARY, before the display list for
+     * this frame is built, so the DL is never interpreted in a mode other
+     * than the one it was built for. */
+    {
+        extern volatile int gSohVRFlatLatch;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRFlatRaw;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRMode;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRRoomMode;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRRoomImage;
+        int sohVRFlat = (gPlayState == NULL) || (gPlayState->pauseCtx.state != 0);
+        /* rev2: is this room IMAGE-BACKED? MeshHeader type 1 is OoT's
+         * pre-rendered-background room shape (z64scene.h / z_room.c's
+         * func_80096F6C). Published unconditionally for `vr contract`. */
+        int sohVRImageRoom = 0;
+        int sohVRFixedCam = 0;
+        if ((gPlayState != NULL) && (gPlayState->roomCtx.curRoom.meshHeader != NULL) &&
+            (gPlayState->roomCtx.curRoom.meshHeader->base.type == 1)) {
+            sohVRImageRoom = 1;
+        }
+        gSohVRRoomImage = sohVRImageRoom;
+        /* rev9 (VR R16 part B): is a FIXED CAMERA in force? The Market's
+         * pre-rendered look is camera data, not room data -- see the patch
+         * header for the o2r scan that says so, for why this is gated on the
+         * town scenes, and for why the setting read here is one tick old.
+         * Published unconditionally, like the room arm, so `vr room` can be
+         * read before VR is entered. */
+        {
+            extern volatile int gSohVRCamSetting;
+            extern volatile int gSohVRRoomFixedCam;
+            /* ticks the raw arm must hold before the latch follows it. The
+             * camera setting changes as Link crosses a region boundary and a
+             * latch that strobed with it would flip the whole frame between
+             * panel and stereo (0031's SIZE hold, same reason). */
+            const int sohVRFixedHold = 5;
+            static int sSohVRFixedRawPrev = 0;
+            static int sSohVRFixedHeld = 0;
+            static int sSohVRFixedState = 0;
+            int sohVRFixedRaw = 0;
+            if (gPlayState != NULL) {
+                s32 sohVRScene = (s32)gPlayState->sceneNum;
+                s32 sohVRSet = (s32)gSohVRCamSetting;
+                if ((sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_ENTRANCE_DAY) || (sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_ENTRANCE_NIGHT) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_ENTRANCE_RUINS) || (sohVRScene == SCENE_BACK_ALLEY_DAY) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_BACK_ALLEY_NIGHT) || (sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_DAY) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_NIGHT) || (sohVRScene == SCENE_MARKET_RUINS) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_TEMPLE_OF_TIME_EXTERIOR_DAY) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_TEMPLE_OF_TIME_EXTERIOR_NIGHT) ||
+                    (sohVRScene == SCENE_TEMPLE_OF_TIME_EXTERIOR_RUINS)) {
+                    if ((sohVRSet == CAM_SET_PREREND_FIXED) || (sohVRSet == CAM_SET_PREREND_PIVOT) ||
+                        (sohVRSet == CAM_SET_PREREND_SIDE_SCROLL) || (sohVRSet == CAM_SET_PIVOT_FROM_SIDE) ||
+                        (sohVRSet == CAM_SET_MARKET_BALCONY)) {
+                        sohVRFixedRaw = 1;
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+            if (sohVRFixedRaw != sSohVRFixedRawPrev) {
+                sSohVRFixedRawPrev = sohVRFixedRaw;
+                sSohVRFixedHeld = 1;
+            } else if (sSohVRFixedHeld < sohVRFixedHold) {
+                sSohVRFixedHeld++;
+            }
+            if (sSohVRFixedHeld >= sohVRFixedHold) {
+                sSohVRFixedState = sSohVRFixedRawPrev;
+            }
+            sohVRFixedCam = sSohVRFixedState;
+            gSohVRRoomFixedCam = sohVRFixedCam;
+        }
+        /* rev2: in VR, with the default "panel" room treatment, an
+         * image-backed room is a flat-screen context: the whole room renders
+         * mono onto the world-locked panel, which is the only way the
+         * authored 2D art reads correctly in stereo. Modes 1 and 2, and every
+         * non-VR build, see exactly rev1's predicate.
+         * rev9: and so is a fixed-camera region of the town scenes, for the
+         * same reason -- the geometry is authored for one angle and the camera
+         * will not follow the head. */
+        if ((gSohVRMode != 0) && (gSohVRRoomMode == 0) && (sohVRImageRoom || sohVRFixedCam)) {
+            sohVRFlat = 1;
+        }
+        gSohVRFlatRaw = sohVRFlat;
+        gSohVRFlatLatch = sohVRFlat;
+        /* rev6 (VR R6): CLEAR THE HAND-MATRIX TABLE HERE, ON THIS THREAD.
+         *
+         * The table (overlay 0050) holds raw graph-pool addresses recorded
+         * during the limb draw, and it must not outlive the session that
+         * recorded them -- the arena reuses those addresses, and the
+         * interpreter's pointer-identity search would then re-seat an
+         * unrelated display-list matrix onto a hand delta. R6 first cleared it
+         * from the compositor loop thread at VR exit, which is the SAME
+         * two-owner mistake as the gSohVROverlayDL crash this round fixed:
+         * every writer of this table is the GAME thread (0042's limb override
+         * sets the tag, 0050's Matrix_ToMtx funnel appends the pointers), and a
+         * draw that had already sampled gSohVRFpActive could still be writing
+         * while the loop zeroed it. Milder than the crash -- the consumers are
+         * bounds-checked, so the damage is one frame of torn hand matrix rather
+         * than a fault -- and wrong for exactly the same reason.
+         *
+         * So the loop thread only asks (gSohVRHandMtxClearReq) and the game
+         * thread clears, here, at the tick boundary before the frame's display
+         * list is built. gSohVRMode == 0 is the backstop, and it is a durable
+         * state: Soh_Exit3DFinalize sets it once, on the main queue this thread
+         * pumps, so a lost request still costs at most a frame.
+         *
+         * gSohVRFpActive is deliberately NOT one of the triggers, and the first
+         * version of this block used it and was measured wrong: it is
+         * recomputed in Play_Draw, i.e. AFTER this block runs, and it is not
+         * stable frame to frame (the far-camera guard alone flips it). Clearing
+         * on it wipes the registrations the previous draw just made -- observed
+         * directly as mtx_n stuck at 0,0 and live_hits 0 in a live VR session
+         * whose pointer search was running 27k times. The table being stale
+         * INSIDE a session is harmless (same graph pool, same addresses); it is
+         * only stale ACROSS sessions that matters, and mode covers that.
+         *
+         * The count goes first, so a draw that catches this mid-clear sees an
+         * empty table rather than a live count over cleared pointers. */
+        {
+            extern volatile int gSohVRHandMtxClearReq;
+            extern volatile int gSohVRHandMtxTag;
+            extern void* volatile gSohVRHandMtxPtr[2][4];
+            extern volatile int gSohVRHandMtxCount[2];
+            extern volatile float gSohVRHandRecMat[2][16];
+            extern volatile int gSohVRHandRecValid[2];
+
+            if ((gSohVRHandMtxClearReq != 0) || (gSohVRMode == 0)) {
+                if ((gSohVRHandMtxTag != 0) || (gSohVRHandMtxCount[0] != 0) || (gSohVRHandMtxCount[1] != 0) ||
+                    (gSohVRHandRecValid[0] != 0) || (gSohVRHandRecValid[1] != 0)) {
+                    s32 sohMtxH;
+                    s32 sohMtxI;
+
+                    gSohVRHandMtxTag = 0;
+                    for (sohMtxH = 0; sohMtxH < 2; sohMtxH++) {
+                        gSohVRHandMtxCount[sohMtxH] = 0;
+                        gSohVRHandRecValid[sohMtxH] = 0;
+                        for (sohMtxI = 0; sohMtxI < 4; sohMtxI++) {
+                            gSohVRHandMtxPtr[sohMtxH][sohMtxI] = NULL;
+                        }
+                        for (sohMtxI = 0; sohMtxI < 16; sohMtxI++) {
+                            gSohVRHandRecMat[sohMtxH][sohMtxI] = 0.0f;
+                        }
+                    }
+                }
+                gSohVRHandMtxClearReq = 0;
+            }
+        }
+        /* rev3 (VR R3, spec D6/D9): the right stick is the TURN in VR, and
+         * only the turn. See the patch header for the full mechanism -- LUS
+         * binds the right stick to the C-buttons as well, at a LOWER threshold
+         * than any turn gesture, which is what wedged Link into item use while
+         * turning kept working. One snapshot, one place, before Play_Update. */
+        if ((gSohVRMode != 0) && (sohVRFlat == 0)) {
+            extern volatile float gSohVRTurnAxis;
+            extern volatile int gSohVRPadCur;
+            extern volatile int gSohVRPadCMasked;
+            extern volatile int gSohVRSensePadBits;
+            Input* sohIn = &gameState->input[0];
+            s32 sohRx = sohIn->cur.right_stick_x;
+            s32 sohRy = sohIn->cur.right_stick_y;
+            /* 25% of MAX_AXIS_RANGE (85) -- the exact provenance test the
+             * SDLAxisDirectionToButtonMapping used to set these bits. */
+            const s32 sohCThresh = 21;
+            u16 sohCFromStick = 0;
+            u16 sohCFromDpad = 0;
+            /* rev5 (VR R5): the bits overlay 0047 OR'd in from the Sense pair
+             * THIS tick. Zero when no pair is connected, when 0047 is not
+             * built, and in every non-VR build -- so the mask below reduces
+             * to rev4's exactly. */
+            u16 sohCFromSense = (u16)((unsigned int)gSohVRSensePadBits & 0xFFFFu);
+            /* rev8 (VR R8 part B): THE CHORD IS GONE, and with it the last
+             * path by which the right stick could produce a C button.
+             *
+             * rev7 made "hold the right grip and the stick becomes the C-pad"
+             * because on a Sense pair the C items had nothing else to live on.
+             * The item wheel (overlay 0051) now lives on that same right grip
+             * and covers the C items properly, and the user's ruling is flat:
+             * "we still have the right joystick sometimes acting like c
+             * buttons... let's move it [the wheel] to the RIGHT GRIP." So the
+             * right stick is the TURN and nothing else, in every mode: X turns,
+             * Y is ignored, and no deflection of it can ever set a C bit.
+             *
+             * What survives from rev3 is the MASK -- LUS's own gamepad
+             * axis-to-button mapping raises C bits from the right stick at a
+             * lower threshold than any turn gesture, and those are cleared here
